---
term: Ecumenical charter
section: structure
order: 75
definition: A charter designed to hold the position of the [[highest human charter]], with an objective broad enough to include members who disagree about most other goods.
seeAlso:
  - highest-human-charter
  - capability
  - limit-of-arbitration
---
The highest human charter faces the Social Charter's motivating question in its hardest form. Its members share fewer interests than the members of any charter below it, so it can't adopt one group's idea of the good as its objective. An ecumenical charter instead pursues the conditions under which its members pursue their own goods.

Which objective serves that purpose is a choice for the charter's design. The coalition that forms the highest human charter makes the choice, and its members can amend or replace the design like any other charter.

The capability approach and its later developments supply the standard candidates. [[Capability]] measures what each member is free to do and be. *Collective capabilities* extend the measure to what people can do only by acting together, through groups and organizations. Peter Evans, Frances Stewart, and Solava Ibrahim developed the idea between 2002 and 2006. A charter that adopts one of these measures can value how its members' capabilities grow over time, as well as their present level.
